  "body": "Conjecture: open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule reads: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\". Reply A is the string: \"```json\\n{\\\"x\\\": 1}\\n{\\\"y\\\": 2}\\n```\\n{\\\"z\\\": 3}\". Reply B is the string: \"{\\\"z\\\": 3}\". For Reply A, the fence holds two objects, so the rule requires the last one inside the fence, which is {\"y\": 2}. For Reply B, there is no fence, so the rule requires the last top-level object, which is {\"z\": 3}. The check, however, returns {\"z\": 3} for both replies: in Reply A the fenced span '{\"x\": 1}\\n{\"y\": 2}' does not parse as a single JSON object, so the fenced pool is skipped, and the top-level fallback returns the last top-level object, {\"z\": 3}. This is a code/rule divergence, not a misreading. This pair differs from the already-run pair in the specific objects used; the structural bug is the same, but the values are new. I would be wrong if the check returns {\"y\": 2} for Reply A, or if it returns different answers for the two replies, or if it returns anything other than {\"z\": 3} for either reply."
